## Skills - trigger table

<!-- 169 skills is too many to choose from reliably. Map PHRASINGS YOU ACTUALLY USE to skill
     names. Two jobs: tells the agent when to stop and read a skill instead of improvising,
     and doubles as a live index of what is relevant to YOUR project. -->

**Check these before improvising a MATLAB/Simulink approach** - if one covers the task, follow it
rather than inventing a method. Read its `SKILL.md` first; only open `references/` or `scripts/`
when `SKILL.md` says to.

| Trigger in the request | Skill |
|---|---|
| "is the model compliant", DO-178C / MISRA / MAB / ISO 26262, Model Advisor run | `checking-model-compliance` |
| Build or edit a Simulink model programmatically | `building-simulink-models` |
| Run/configure a simulation | `simulating-simulink-models` |
| Write or run model tests | `testing-simulink-models`, `authoring-simulink-inputs` |
| Fault injection, safety analysis | `inject-faults`, `manage-safety-analysis` |
| "write a spec / test plan / implementation plan" for an algorithm | `specifying-mbd-algorithms` |
| Project paths broken, model refs, add files to project | `managing-simulink-projects` |
| "wrong value at t=X", step through a sim | `simulink-debug-commandline` |
| "simulation is slow", compare two runs | `simulink-profiler-analyzer` |
| "model takes forever to load/compile" | `simulink-profile-initialization` |
| Solver errors, step size, zero-crossings, algebraic loops | `simulink-solver-profiler-analyzer` |
| "file a bug", "document this defect" | `filing-bug-reports` |

### Always-applicable idioms

<!-- Keep these as plain .md files WITHOUT YAML frontmatter so Claude Code does not surface
     them as invocable skills. They encode things that produce working-but-WRONG results
     if skipped. -->

Read the relevant note before writing any MATLAB that touches a model:

- **Interaction** - "this block" = `gcb`, "this model" = `bdroot`, "this subsystem" = `gcs`
- **Modeling** - use `add_block` + `Simulink.BlockDiagram.arrangeSystem`; **never** hand-position
  blocks with pixel coordinates
- **Simulation** - **always** `out = sim(in)` with a `Simulink.SimulationInput`; **never**
  `set_param` for simulation parameters - that mutates the model file instead of configuring a run
- **Baseline** - capture a baseline **before** modifying any model, then verify against it

## MATLAB MCP

Configured in `.mcp.json` as server name `matlab` (project scope, stdio).
**Restart Claude Code after editing it** - MCP servers load at session start.

Prefer the `model_*` tools over hand-written `set_param`/`get_param` - they are what the skills
assume, and mixing approaches produces advice that doesn't match what then happens.
Cold start is ≈20–30 s for the first call; later calls in the same session are fast.

Facts worth not rediscovering:

- MATLAB root `<C:\Program Files\MATLAB\R2026a>`. Binary is the toolkit's
  `~/.matlab/agentic-toolkits/bin/matlab-mcp-server.exe`.
- `startup.m` runs `satk_initialize` + `addpath(genpath(...))` automatically, because the server
  starts MATLAB here. **Without `satk_initialize` every `model_*` tool fails** with "Unrecognized
  function or variable". If you start MATLAB manually from another folder, run it yourself.
- `--log-folder=<C:/mcplog>` is **deliberate, not cosmetic.** The server opens a unix-domain socket
  inside its log folder; long paths fail with `socket path is too long`, and `%TEMP%` failed with
  `access denied for socket file`. Keep a short, writable, local folder.
- `--matlab-session-mode=auto` is deliberate too. `setupAgenticToolkit` writes `existing`, which
  only works when a MATLAB with `satk_initialize` is *already* running; `auto` falls back to
  starting one.
- **Never run `setupAgenticToolkit("configure")` from an agent session.** It prompts with `input()`;
  every MCP call arrives as an empty line producing "Invalid selection", and it loops forever. No
  tool call can rescue it - a human must press Ctrl-C in the MATLAB desktop. It also overwrites
  `.mcp.json` wholesale; a backup is at `<tools/.mcp.json.bak>`.

## Version control

- **`.slx` files cannot be merged.** `.gitattributes` marks them `binary`, which expands to
  `-diff -merge -text`, so Git refuses to auto-merge and the only resolution is "pick one side and
  redo the other side's work by hand in Simulink." **Avoid the situation rather than resolving it:**
  put logic in `.m` files, hold one model at a time, keep branches short.
- **Before any `model_edit`**, confirm the tree is clean and committed - `model_edit` is destructive
  and a binary `.slx` edit cannot be diffed or undone. `git stash`/commit first; never assume a
  restore point exists.
- <Branch protection / PR policy.>

Named restore points:

| Ref | What |
|---|---|
| `<baseline/YYYY-MM-DD-name>` | <what state this captures> |

## Conventions

- <e.g. MATLAB functions are `%#codegen` and written for embedded code generation: fixed-size
  arrays, no dynamic allocation, no cell arrays. Keep new code codegen-safe.>
- <e.g. Header comments cite the governing requirement or spec section. Preserve and update those
  citations when editing - they are the traceability evidence.>
- <e.g. your file/document naming scheme, if there is one.>

## Do not touch

<!-- Without this the agent will helpfully "fix" things that regenerate, or hand-edit an
     output whose source is elsewhere. -->

- `resources/project/**` - MATLAB project metadata (generated XML). **Committed deliberately** -
  `openProject` fails without it.
- `<docs/diagrams/*.svg>` - **generated**. Edit `<docs/diagrams/src/*.py>` and regenerate; never
  hand-edit the output.
- `skills/**` - vendor-authored. Read and follow them; don't edit. Project-specific procedures
  belong in a new file, not inside a vendor skill.
- <The `archive/...` branch - a frozen record. Never merge it.>

## Maintenance - keep README.md current (mandatory)

<!-- The rule that pays off most. An agent session has no memory of last week. A logbook that
     explains WHY a decision was made is the difference between an agent that reinforces your
     architecture and one that relitigates it every session. -->

`README.md` is the only documentation this repository ships. **Never finish a piece of work without
updating it in the same commit** - not a follow-up one.

### 1. The logbook is not optional

Add an entry for anything substantive:

```
| **YYYY-MM-DD** | **Short title.** What changed and why. |
```

- **Say why, not what.** `git log` already records what. The logbook exists for the reasoning that
  would otherwise evaporate - the constraint discovered, the option rejected, the measurement that
  settled it. *"Scoped the CloseFcn to the project root"* is weak; *"…it was deleting `.slxc`
  relative to MATLAB's current folder, demonstrated by destroying a decoy in an unrelated tree"*
  is the entry worth having.
- **Cite evidence** - test counts, spec IDs, measured numbers. A claim that something works without
  saying how that was established is noise.
- **Record corrections.** When a documented fact turns out wrong, that is the *most* valuable entry.
  **Never silently overwrite a wrong fact.**
- **Never record a result you have not run.** If a claim needs MATLAB and MATLAB is unavailable, say
  so in the entry instead of assuming.
- **Skip trivia** - formatting, comment typos, model layout tidying. One entry per day per theme,
  not per commit.

### 2. The rest of README that goes stale

| When you change… | Update… |
|---|---|
| Add/remove/rename a file or folder | Layout |
| Add or change a `verify_*.m` | The test-count table |
| A new codegen/naming rule | Conventions |
| Resolve or discover a blocker | Open threads |
| Add a tag or branch worth keeping | Named restore points |

### 3. Verify before you write it down

Don't document a behaviour you haven't observed. If it needs MATLAB and MATLAB is unavailable, say
so rather than assuming.

### 4. This file

If `CLAUDE.md` is gitignored, it never reaches a clone. Anything a human collaborator must know
belongs in a tracked file as well - duplication beats a rule nobody receives.
